WEBVTT FILE

﻿1
00:00:09.134 --> 00:00:10.552
This has been a long time in coming.

2
00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:15.015
You know, we're having this conversation
after 21, 22 years

3
00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:17.976
of thinking about this.

4
00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:21.771
Your eyes are burning and you smell it.

5
00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:24.107
If there’s dead fish around.

6
00:00:24.107 --> 00:00:25.900
Your day goes from being high

7
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.987
aspirations and catching lots of fish
to well, we're not going fishing today.

8
00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:31.364
There's nothing to catch,
I don’t have bait.

9
00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:32.574
Can't get through the red tide.

10
00:00:32.574 --> 00:00:35.577
Everything's dead.

11
00:00:35.785 --> 00:00:37.954
Red tide is a nightmare.

12
00:00:37.954 --> 00:00:41.458
Red tide is a problem for fishermen.

13
00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:45.045
It's a problem for people with respiratory
issues that live on the beach.

14
00:00:45.295 --> 00:00:49.090
It's a problem for people that
go to the beach to get their zen time.

15
00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:52.093
Just problematic.

16
00:00:52.093 --> 00:00:55.889
The red tide when it's bad,
you just can't really get away from it.

17
00:00:56.681 --> 00:00:59.684
I mean and if the sea
and the oceans are dead.

18
00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:00.643
What are we going to eat?

19
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.538
I've never been more excited

20
00:01:20.538 --> 00:01:24.834
about the possibility of discovery.

21
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.755
I have 100% confidence

22
00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:32.675
that what we're going to produce from PACE
will surpass

23
00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:35.929
all of our wildest dreams.

24
00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.183
This is going to change the world.

25
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.702
First red tide

26
00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:43.705
I remember was back in the mid 80s.

27
00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:48.126
Don't remember much
before that being all that bad.

28
00:02:48.126 --> 00:02:51.880
But in the 80s, there I was driving
test boats at the Mercury facility.

29
00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.090
And I was in a little slow boat,

30
00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.635
and the dead fish of all species

31
00:02:58.386 --> 00:03:01.389
were floating on top of the water. And,

32
00:03:02.557 --> 00:03:05.560
that was one of the first ones
and one of the worst ones I can remember.

33
00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.395
I remember

34
00:03:07.395 --> 00:03:10.356
just people complaining on the beach.

35
00:03:11.149 --> 00:03:14.152
No it's a bad deal.

36
00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:17.322
What we normally do is we
we run off shore and catch

37
00:03:17.322 --> 00:03:20.325
grouper
and snapper on a private boat that I run.

38
00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:25.079
clients would arrive between seven, 8:00.

39
00:03:26.122 --> 00:03:29.834
We run offshore, we fish all day,
try to get back to the dock by four

40
00:03:29.834 --> 00:03:33.755
because we aspire to have an outstanding
catch every day,

41
00:03:33.755 --> 00:03:35.924
which means spending time at the fish
cleaning table.

42
00:03:35.924 --> 00:03:38.593
So you get home exhausted.

43
00:03:38.593 --> 00:03:39.093
You're tired.

44
00:03:39.093 --> 00:03:41.179
It's. It's 12 hour day, no matter what.

45
00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:42.889
I mean, it's

46
00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:45.892
it's, it's a lot of work,
but it's very rewarding.

47
00:03:49.145 --> 00:03:52.148
Well, it's my life and it's everything.

48
00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.067
And we live in Florida.

49
00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:57.278
People come here to go on the water,
catch fish and enjoy the beaches.

50
00:03:58.238 --> 00:04:00.365
And that's not happening
when there's red tide and death

51
00:04:00.365 --> 00:04:03.368
all over the beach.

52
00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:04.661
We're lucky right now.

53
00:04:04.661 --> 00:04:08.122
We don't have the red tide,
but it runs people off.

54
00:04:20.301 --> 00:04:23.137
I would like to think
we could prevent red tide from happening,

55
00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.348
but that's almost

56
00:04:25.348 --> 00:04:28.351
like trying to keep cancer from happening.

57
00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:30.728
It's a tough one.

58
00:04:30.728 --> 00:04:33.564
You never know where it's going to pop up.

59
00:04:33.564 --> 00:04:35.108
I hope and pray that we can do

60
00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:38.111
something to turn us around here,
because it’s terrible,

61
00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:40.029
absolutely terrible.

62
00:04:40.029 --> 00:04:42.573
And, hopefully
NASA, can get something going

63
00:04:42.573 --> 00:04:45.576
there.

64
00:05:19.569 --> 00:05:22.030
The red tide can be so intense

65
00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:25.033
right along the beach that really
no one is on the beach.

66
00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:28.995
That's further magnified
if the red tide is also

67
00:05:29.245 --> 00:05:32.623
causing
low oxygen levels and caused, fish kills.

68
00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:36.127
And so when you have, 80 pound,

69
00:05:36.127 --> 00:05:41.382
120 pound grouper
or manatee, dead on the beach, rotting,

70
00:05:41.799 --> 00:05:45.553
you now have, biomass,
you have rotting animals.

71
00:05:45.928 --> 00:05:48.931
And that ammonia smell of rotting animals,

72
00:05:49.724 --> 00:05:52.435
and rotting mammals and fish on the beach.

73
00:05:52.435 --> 00:05:54.896
The combination can
truly make the beach just,

74
00:05:56.564 --> 00:05:59.525
not tolerable for anyone.

75
00:06:00.818 --> 00:06:04.322
Since I've worked here,
we had a period of about 18 months

76
00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:07.325
where red tide was on and off nonstop.

77
00:06:07.325 --> 00:06:10.328
It pretty much shut down the island.

78
00:06:12.789 --> 00:06:15.041
We had a large influx of patients.

79
00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:17.794
It was pretty much a daily occurrence.

80
00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:20.797
Somebody
calling with a respiratory problem.

81
00:06:22.256 --> 00:06:24.217
We have a hard time telling people

82
00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:27.220
to limit your exposure.

83
00:06:28.304 --> 00:06:31.307
So it isolated a lot of people
at that time.

84
00:06:35.228 --> 00:06:38.356
I think that the more data we have
and the more science,

85
00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:42.151
and the more funding we can direct
towards science to better understand

86
00:06:42.443 --> 00:06:45.822
red tide, then the more likely we are
to be able to mitigate and take steps

87
00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:49.826
to at least make some measurement
of improvement in controlling,

88
00:06:50.076 --> 00:06:53.746
predicting when it's going to be bad,
but also controlling just how bad it is

89
00:06:54.163 --> 00:06:58.167
and lessening the suffering not only of
us, but also of our marine friends.

90
00:07:01.421 --> 00:07:03.881
So you have to elevate your senses
all the way into space.

91
00:07:03.881 --> 00:07:07.844
So only from a satellite point of view
do you get this

92
00:07:07.844 --> 00:07:11.722
beautiful synoptic,
picture of our home planet.

93
00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:16.185
And if you weren't able to do that,
there would be so many parts

94
00:07:16.185 --> 00:07:19.480
of the role of phytoplankton
that just would otherwise be invisible.

95
00:07:21.399 --> 00:07:24.402
Most people don't realize this, but

96
00:07:25.153 --> 00:07:27.613
NASA plays a foundational role

97
00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:30.908
in the entire international study

98
00:07:30.908 --> 00:07:33.911
of Earth science.

99
00:07:34.036 --> 00:07:35.705
So I knew that

100
00:07:35.705 --> 00:07:38.708
I wanted to be involved in marine science,

101
00:07:39.041 --> 00:07:40.835
since eighth grade.

102
00:07:40.835 --> 00:07:44.630
And I was lucky enough at the time,
the public school that I attended in

103
00:07:45.131 --> 00:07:48.342
Central Connecticut had a program
that brought me

104
00:07:48.342 --> 00:07:51.804
to the biological station
for research in Bermuda.

105
00:07:52.305 --> 00:07:55.308
And that was a transformative moment.

106
00:07:57.101 --> 00:08:01.022
And it's not just learning
how the satellite system

107
00:08:01.022 --> 00:08:05.151
relates to the oceanography
I was already interested in, but,

108
00:08:05.151 --> 00:08:05.735
good grief,

109
00:08:05.735 --> 00:08:10.323
I mean, the greatest collection of Earth
scientists in the country reside here.

110
00:08:15.786 --> 00:08:16.829
Well, all right, so the ocean

111
00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:20.374
provides it,
you know, provides a food source.

112
00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:22.710
It helps regulate weather and climate.

113
00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:27.006
There are compounds from the ocean
that make medicines and jobs economy.

114
00:08:27.632 --> 00:08:30.676
All of that ties back to
what is in the ocean.

115
00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:33.846
And so from a phytoplankton point of view,
you have the beneficial ones

116
00:08:33.846 --> 00:08:37.350
that feed fisheries, the beneficial ones
that are moving carbon around

117
00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:38.226
and influencing climate.

118
00:08:38.226 --> 00:08:41.229
There are also the harmful ones.

119
00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:45.191
Some of these are harmful enough
that these phytoplankton could kill you

120
00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:48.694
and they're the ones that form red tides,

121
00:08:48.903 --> 00:08:53.574
where you're seeing beach closures
or oyster farms

122
00:08:53.574 --> 00:08:56.744
suddenly having to be closed
because of contamination.

123
00:08:58.621 --> 00:09:02.458
They carry this particular chemical
that tends to be an irritant

124
00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:06.796
to humans and animals
or create an odor and so forth.

125
00:09:09.632 --> 00:09:12.426
It's not enough just to say I see
phytoplankton there.

126
00:09:12.426 --> 00:09:15.388
You know, if you're sitting on the West
Florida shelf, for example,

127
00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:18.432
and you see a bloom of phytoplankton
from space, it's important to know

128
00:09:18.641 --> 00:09:23.062
whether it's a red tide
that might have some severe impact to that

129
00:09:23.062 --> 00:09:26.857
coastal community, versus it just happens
to be a lot of phytoplankton

130
00:09:26.857 --> 00:09:29.318
there.

131
00:09:29.318 --> 00:09:30.486
Some of these form

132
00:09:30.486 --> 00:09:33.573
in places like the, the Great Lakes.

133
00:09:33.573 --> 00:09:39.579
And there was an instance not long ago
where a cyanotoxin bloom,

134
00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:42.832
shut down the water
intake to the city of Toledo.

135
00:09:43.499 --> 00:09:47.295
And this is happening on more diverse

136
00:09:47.295 --> 00:09:49.714
areas, places
where they weren't happening before.

137
00:09:49.714 --> 00:09:51.882
Sometimes they're happening
with more frequency.

138
00:09:51.882 --> 00:09:53.050
Anyway.

139
00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:55.636
Point is, is that we want to monitor them,

140
00:09:55.636 --> 00:09:57.930
and that's where PACE comes in.

141
00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:58.931
We have been doing ocean

142
00:09:58.931 --> 00:10:02.393
color from space and atmospheric work
from space for over 40 years.

143
00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:06.397
And we have this beautiful fleet at NASA
and with our international partners,

144
00:10:07.315 --> 00:10:09.734
that really have formed the foundation
for what

145
00:10:09.734 --> 00:10:12.612
PACE Observatory is going to be. And

146
00:10:13.613 --> 00:10:14.780
they're wonderful at

147
00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:19.577
doing their job,
which was or is to separate

148
00:10:19.577 --> 00:10:22.955
a signal from phytoplankton
from everything else in the water column.

149
00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:26.083
But what they don't do well is

150
00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:28.502
tell you what is contributing

151
00:10:28.502 --> 00:10:31.505
to that biomass,
what community is actually there.

152
00:10:31.589 --> 00:10:35.509
And that's because, they use ocean color,
but they're only looking at 7

153
00:10:35.509 --> 00:10:37.094
or 8 colors of the rainbow.

154
00:10:37.094 --> 00:10:41.057
And they do that by having measurements
of several blue bands,

155
00:10:41.057 --> 00:10:44.060
several green bands,
and several red bands.

156
00:10:44.226 --> 00:10:47.521
But that's still eight
crayons in your box.

157
00:10:49.065 --> 00:10:51.400
If you wanted to actually see
the subtleties

158
00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:54.403
and different greens and different blues,
you need more color.

159
00:10:54.779 --> 00:10:59.075
PACE is full spectrum, full rainbow
from the ultraviolet to the near-infrared.

160
00:10:59.408 --> 00:11:02.953
So you would imagine this as being handed
a box of 200 crayons.

161
00:11:02.953 --> 00:11:07.541
And if I asked you to paint, sorry, color
the most spectacular landscape

162
00:11:07.541 --> 00:11:09.210
you could, which box are you gonna choose?

163
00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:11.379
You want all the color,
you don’t want some of the colors.

164
00:11:15.466 --> 00:11:19.261
So the
information content for any singular

165
00:11:19.261 --> 00:11:22.264
instrument on PACE is way
more than we're used to.

166
00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:26.143
This is such a cool problem to have.

167
00:11:27.103 --> 00:11:31.691
So suddenly the tools you have at
your fingertips

168
00:11:31.691 --> 00:11:34.694
will fill in all the gaps of color
that we could possibly want.

169
00:11:36.987 --> 00:11:39.073
We know the atmosphere is changing

170
00:11:39.073 --> 00:11:42.910
every time
a pattern in agriculture changes,

171
00:11:43.452 --> 00:11:47.206
you know, is there something that's
altering nutrient availability

172
00:11:47.206 --> 00:11:51.711
to your local marine ecosystem that is
suddenly changing how things are growing?

173
00:11:52.169 --> 00:11:56.674
You may be noticing in your backyard
if you have a small body of water,

174
00:11:56.674 --> 00:12:01.971
why are these scums of algae forming
that weren't there a few years ago?

175
00:12:01.971 --> 00:12:04.974
And then they disappear
and the timings are changing.

176
00:12:06.308 --> 00:12:08.352
We know carbon dioxide levels

177
00:12:08.352 --> 00:12:11.772
have increased over the past 200 years.

178
00:12:17.653 --> 00:12:20.656
We know that has warmed our atmosphere.

179
00:12:24.368 --> 00:12:25.661
A warming atmosphere,

180
00:12:25.661 --> 00:12:29.206
the physics behind it are starting
to change how our oceans behave.

181
00:12:29.206 --> 00:12:31.375
Circulation patterns are changing.

182
00:12:31.375 --> 00:12:34.378
Places are warmer than they used to be.

183
00:12:35.296 --> 00:12:38.466
Currents are evolving

184
00:12:38.716 --> 00:12:41.969
to bring nutrients to places
they once were not.

185
00:12:46.807 --> 00:12:49.727
In many ways, you know you
you wish you would have had these eyes

186
00:12:49.727 --> 00:12:53.689
in the sky forever because you know
that information is so powerful.

187
00:12:57.067 --> 00:13:00.070
So there's a lot of benefit
from an instrument like OCI,

188
00:13:00.196 --> 00:13:03.866
where you can reveal phytoplankton
features and algae features

189
00:13:03.866 --> 00:13:07.244
that you just can't see with
the instruments that we have in orbit now.

190
00:13:11.916 --> 00:13:15.711
It is going to provide
really a quantum leap

191
00:13:16.128 --> 00:13:17.505
in the knowledge base

192
00:13:17.505 --> 00:13:21.091
that we have, it's going to change
our foundational knowledge of things.

193
00:13:21.217 --> 00:13:24.220
And we are going to stumble
into new discoveries

194
00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:27.556
that, you know, I can't predict.

195
00:13:29.683 --> 00:13:31.227
The economy is tied to it

196
00:13:31.227 --> 00:13:35.189
now, not just coastal economies, but
it's easy to think of it in a coastal way.

197
00:13:35.189 --> 00:13:36.607
You know that there are fishermen,

198
00:13:36.607 --> 00:13:39.652
there are shell fishermen,
there's recreation associated with this.

199
00:13:40.152 --> 00:13:43.030
So if you enjoy, you know, eating

200
00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:45.950
or you enjoy going to the beach
or being out on the water, phytoplankton

201
00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:48.953
play a big role in your life,
whether you know it or not.

202
00:13:54.083 --> 00:13:58.003
So the scientific community, which is
inclusive of partners like NOAA, the U.S.

203
00:13:58.003 --> 00:13:59.505
Environmental Protection Agency

204
00:13:59.505 --> 00:14:02.508
and other organizations like that
that do watershed management

205
00:14:02.591 --> 00:14:05.886
or have some responsibility
for safe and clean drinking water.

206
00:14:06.554 --> 00:14:10.182
They spend a lot of time
thinking about, the frequency,

207
00:14:10.307 --> 00:14:14.103
the duration and the extent
of these harmful algal blooms.

208
00:14:15.354 --> 00:14:17.857
and I've been involved in a project,
actually, that's really fascinating

209
00:14:17.857 --> 00:14:20.109
about using satellite data

210
00:14:20.109 --> 00:14:24.154
as a tool to start looking at longer
term time series of these.

211
00:14:24.196 --> 00:14:28.409
So I don't know that
anybody was specifically predicting a boom

212
00:14:28.409 --> 00:14:32.621
or bust of them over time,
but they certainly are important enough,

213
00:14:32.621 --> 00:14:35.958
and resources to monitor
them are scarce enough

214
00:14:36.542 --> 00:14:40.087
that using satellite information
to start studying harmful

215
00:14:40.087 --> 00:14:44.049
algal blooms has been really,
really, popular and,

216
00:14:44.425 --> 00:14:47.887
becoming more of the forefront of
that field, at least from a

217
00:14:49.638 --> 00:14:50.890
management point of view over

218
00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:53.893
the past ten years or so.

219
00:14:56.437 --> 00:15:00.232
And so the real origin of the PACE mission

220
00:15:01.525 --> 00:15:03.402
is circa 1999

221
00:15:03.402 --> 00:15:07.323
2000.
where SeaWiFS was flying and successful.

222
00:15:07.323 --> 00:15:09.950
MODIS Terra had just launched.

223
00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:12.661
And at that point
the ocean color community

224
00:15:12.661 --> 00:15:15.664
had a whole new playbook
for their research.

225
00:15:16.123 --> 00:15:20.002
And those missions are so successful,
the scientific community

226
00:15:20.002 --> 00:15:23.714
really started diving into, well,
if we had to do this again,

227
00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:26.967
what happens now
and what does that look like?

228
00:15:26.967 --> 00:15:32.556
And that evolved into a series of studies
conducted at Goddard

229
00:15:32.556 --> 00:15:35.976
and elsewhere with collaborators
from all over the country.

230
00:15:37.186 --> 00:15:40.147
And the genesis of the idea

231
00:15:40.397 --> 00:15:43.400
became a little bit more real.

232
00:16:12.346 --> 00:16:13.597
It would be so profound

233
00:16:13.597 --> 00:16:18.310
to have a new ability
to look at this blue marble.

234
00:16:18.519 --> 00:16:19.812
You know, we're all citizens of Earth.

235
00:16:19.812 --> 00:16:22.940
Why don't we want to be watching
it every day from space?

236
00:16:45.838 --> 00:16:48.173
Now we are

237
00:16:48.173 --> 00:16:51.176
10.5 hours from launch.

238
00:17:19.038 --> 00:17:22.624
The deeper dive
we take into the relationships

239
00:17:22.875 --> 00:17:27.546
between land, ocean, and atmosphere,
the more realize we don't know

240
00:17:27.546 --> 00:17:30.549
and we need to learn.

241
00:17:31.842 --> 00:17:34.970
It's going to revolutionize
and allow us to take

242
00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:38.766
this huge volume of information
that we've never had before.

243
00:17:39.099 --> 00:17:42.936
And actually dissect it and integrate it
in really, really interesting ways.

244
00:17:44.855 --> 00:17:46.523
We're going to start learning

245
00:17:46.523 --> 00:17:50.444
causes and effects or just interactions,

246
00:17:50.444 --> 00:17:53.530
and how different parts of the ecosystem
start piecing

247
00:17:53.530 --> 00:17:56.533
together.

248
00:17:59.328 --> 00:18:00.829
So I can say

249
00:18:00.829 --> 00:18:06.460
without hesitation that the new data
coming out of PACE from all three of its

250
00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:11.215
instruments is going to absolutely change
the way we view our home planet.

251
00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:15.135
And what will come with that is a deeper
understanding of how all the systems

252
00:18:15.135 --> 00:18:20.140
connect, why things are happening,
why decisions we might make

253
00:18:21.391 --> 00:18:23.769
could influence other things downstream

254
00:18:23.769 --> 00:18:26.772
in the grand sense
of how everything fits together.

255
00:18:58.512 --> 00:19:00.055
Ten. Nine.

256
00:19:00.055 --> 00:19:02.975
Eight. Seven. Six.

257
00:19:02.975 --> 00:19:05.018
Five. Four.

258
00:19:05.018 --> 00:19:07.771
Three. Two. One.

259
00:19:07.771 --> 00:19:10.023
Booster ignition.

260
00:19:10.023 --> 00:19:13.360
Full power
engines and liftoff of the Falcon

261
00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:16.363
Nine and PACE.

262
00:19:50.772 --> 00:19:53.775
1.7 billion pounds of thrust.

263
00:20:08.373 --> 00:20:11.376
Come on girl!

264
00:20:42.366 --> 00:20:43.825
Looking inside.

265
00:20:43.825 --> 00:20:49.748
There go the fairings.
